			<div class="content" id="post-content-2789461">
									

<p>I can see the thematic point, but I'm afraid this wasn't scary of itself - not least because if the Foundation has science sufficiently worked out to allow time-travel for the infection of early humans with an engineered brain parasite that won't affect cognition other than activating fear (and the unintended consequence risks of that are immense), then to be honest I don't think they need to be scared of anything. Particularly when this boogeyman doesn't even cast a shadow on the text.</p>
<p>It's all a little too pat and convenient for me, and the final note (again, the final note trope) keeps circling around the same points like it's trying to find a killer finishing metaphor, rather than just explaining what's going on in a clearly understandable way. I'm afraid my suspension of belief was destroyed.</p>

